  "result": "Default `[]` evaluated once, at `def` time, so every call shares one list object. `append` mutates it, so `add(2)` sees `[1]` from before and returns `[1, 2]`.\n\nFix: use `None` sentinel, make fresh list per call.\n\n```python\ndef add(x, items=None):\n    if items is None:\n        items = []\n    items.append(x)\n    return items\n```\n\nCheck:\n\n```python\nadd(1)  # [1]\nadd(2)  # [2]\n```\n\nSame applies to `{}`, `set()`, other mutable defaults.",
